// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// BrainSpark Horizon — curated technology foresight register.
//
// The knowledge moat behind /horizon: technologies across all commodities
// and powertrains, each positioned with an automotive TRL (APC 1–9), a rough
// current adoption share of its applicable segment, named production evidence,
// drivers, cost-trend direction and leading players. The deterministic cores
// in foresight.mjs turn these into S-curve phases, horizon buckets, adoption
// projections and momentum scores — the LLM only narrates on top.
//
// Plain .mjs + hand-written .d.mts (commodity-classify pattern) so server and
// frontend share one source; tests/foresight.test.mjs enforces entry validity.
//
// Curation rules (keep the register honest):
//  • trl:        where the tech stands TODAY in automotive productionisation.
//  • adoptionPct: share of the applicable segment's current production, not
//                 fleet; rough single digits are fine, precision is fake.
//  • firstProduction: cite a real programme + year, or omit entirely.
//  • regAnchor:  only when a listed regulation genuinely forces/pulls the tech.
//  • matchTerms: lowercase part-name fragments users actually type.
//  • GLOBAL FRONTIER FIRST (2026 lesson): before writing any spec/gauge/
//    performance claim, check the Chinese production frontier — the West is
//    often not the leading edge (0.15mm laminations shipped in Xiaomi motors
//    while this register still said 0.20mm). `npm run horizon:audit` flags
//    entries with no China-based reference; clear the flag with evidence or
//    a deliberate reason, never by ignoring it.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

